<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">North Peruvian Republic</h1><p class="st-meta">1836 CE – 1839 CE · capital Lima</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The northern Peruvian state, ruled from Lima, that formed part of the Peru-Bolivian Confederation between 1836 and 1839.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Following the creation of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/south-peruvian-republic">South Peruvian Republic</a>, an assembly of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">departments</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-libertad-region">La Libertad</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/junin-region">Junín</a>, met at Huaura in August 1836 and created the North Peruvian Republic. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-jose-de-orbegoso">Luis José de Orbegoso</a>, who had invited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-de-santa-cruz">Andrés de Santa Cruz</a> into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, became its president.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The republic joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peru-bolivian-confederation">Peru-Bolivian Confederation</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-de-santa-cruz">Santa Cruz</a> as Supreme Protector. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lima">Lima</a> elites resented what they saw as Bolivian domination and the division of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a>, and Peruvian exiles in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Chile</a> campaigned against it. In 1838, as a Chilean expedition advanced, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-jose-de-orbegoso">Orbegoso</a> broke with Santa Cruz and declared the North Peruvian Republic independent of the confederation, but was defeated by the Chileans outside Lima.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-yungay">Yungay</a> in January 1839 ended the confederation, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agustin-gamarra">Agustín Gamarra</a> restored a unified <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-peru">Republic of Peru</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luis-jose-de-orbegoso">Orbegoso</a> went into exile, and a constitution adopted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huancayo">Huancayo</a> in 1839 strengthened central government. The episode deepened the rivalry between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peru">Peru</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Chile</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/north-peruvian-republic.jpg" alt="North Peruvian Republic" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: MiguelAlanCS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Peru_(1825%E2%80%931884).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1836 CE – 1839 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lima</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Luis José de Orbegoso</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/peru">Peru</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Republic of North Peru, Estado Nor-Peruano</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=north-peruvian-republic" aria-label="North Peruvian Republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2563 2627 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where North Peruvian Republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2883 2883L3095 3103M2883 2883L2883 2883"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3095 3103h0M2883 2883h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2883 2883h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2883 2883h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1838&amp;focus=north-peruvian-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
